8551: bibliography and manifest name a candidate branch/path coordinate, but no finite
8552: record in the current package proves that the public bytes equal the final digest
8553: rows.  These finite statements determine all four fields in the displayed table, while
8554: Proposition~\ref{prop:upload-or-archive-instantiation-update-protocol} specifies
8555: the finite manuscript, manifest, bibliography, and digest updates needed to leave
8556: this normal form in a later package.
8557: 
8558: If the last field were instead marked externally instantiated, the record would
8559: contain neither of the two coordinates that Theorem~\ref{thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary}
8560: requires.  That would add a support coordinate not present in the package,
8561: contradicting Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  Hence
8562: the only compatible normal form is the one displayed, and its consequence for
8563: bundle-backed claims follows from
8564: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}.
8565: \end{proof}
8566: 
8567: \begin{corollary}[Current local-support normal form]
8568: \label{cor:current-local-support-normal-form}
8569: For the current presentation package, the finite support state has exactly the
8570: following normal form.  The theorem-inventory coordinate is accepted only through
8571: the post-regeneration digest-and-verifier fixed point of
8572: Proposition~\ref{prop:post-inventory-fixed-point-support-certificate}; the
8573: review-bundle coordinate is externally instantiated only under exact upload or
8574: matching public branch/path bytes, because
8575: Proposition~\ref{prop:submission-support-instantiation-field-normal-form}
8576: records no upload receipt and makes the archive-equivalent locator
8577: byte-conditioned; and the venue and
8578: bibliography coordinate is only the dated local-readiness record of
8579: Proposition~\ref{prop:round-five-venue-record-instantiation}, not an upload-time
8580: venue-compliance certificate.  Consequently every bundle-backed theorem-ledger,
8581: case-evidence, verifier-log, source-interface, and primary-claim-inventory claim
8582: in this package is a local/path-verified exact-inclusion claim unless a later
8583: submitted support surface adds both an accepted upload-or-archive instantiation
8584: record and, for venue compliance, an upload-time or unchanged-rule venue record.
8585: \end{corollary}
8586: 
8587: \begin{proof}
8588: The three named coordinates are disjoint finite fields of the support record.
8589: For the theorem-inventory field,
8590: Proposition~\ref{prop:post-inventory-fixed-point-support-certificate} gives the
8591: necessary and sufficient local fixed point after the regenerated inventory files:
8592: the current byte rows for \path{theorem_inventory.json} and
8593: \path{theorem_inventory.md}, together with the three current finite-record
8594: verifier logs, are the only support coordinates read by the exact-inclusion
8595: ledger.  Replacing any of those rows by a pre-regeneration row leaves only the
8596: manual local index and fails the exact-inclusion theorem-ledger claim.
8597: 
8598: For the submission-instantiation field,
8599: Proposition~\ref{prop:submission-support-instantiation-field-normal-form} lists
8600: the complete current values: conditional exact supplemental inclusion, absent
8601: upload receipt, a digest-conditioned public branch/path locator, and external
8602: support status only when exact upload or matching branch bytes are present.  By
8603: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-no-receipt-support-surface-non-escalation}, no
8604: bundle-local path can therefore be promoted to an unconditional public or
8605: venue-submitted coordinate in this package.  For the venue field,
8606: Proposition~\ref{prop:round-five-venue-record-instantiation} supplies only the
8607: dated 2026-06-09 live route and bibliography check and explicitly excludes
8608: venue upload, acceptance, proceedings status, archive minting, Lean rebuilds,
8609: and artifact reruns.  Corollary~\ref{cor:historical-venue-record-non-escalation}
8610: then prevents that dated record from being read as upload-time compliance unless
8611: a later upload-time tuple or unchanged-rule statement is included.
8612: 
8613: The finite-coordinate determination theorem applies to the product of these
8614: fields.  A support reading that adds external submission support without the
8615: receipt-or-locator field, or adds upload-time venue compliance without the
8616: required venue field, introduces a coordinate absent from the current record.
8617: Thus the only current normal form is the local/path-verified exact-inclusion one
8618: stated above; leaving it requires the finite update protocols already named in
8619: the statement.
8620: \end{proof}
8621: 
8622: \begin{proposition}[Review-bundle availability condition]
8623: \label{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}
8624: The final support mode selected for the current presentation package is exact
8625: supplemental inclusion of \path{review_bundle/}, conditional on the external
8626: instantiation record of Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}, as recorded in
8627: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} and in
8628: Audit record~\ref{prop:final-immutable-package-state-certificate}.  This is a
8629: conditional support-mode selection, not a completed venue-upload record or public
8630: archive locator.  The source-interface, case-snapshot, primary-claim inventory,
8631: submission-interface map, theorem-inventory, and verifier-record claims are
8632: therefore reviewable at the path-verified evidence level stated in this
8633: supplement under the local exact bundle; they become external support only once an
8634: external instantiation record exists.  A future archival equivalent would have to satisfy
8635: Audit record~\ref{audit:review-bundle-archive-equivalence} and the common-field
8636: update protocol of
8637: Proposition~\ref{prop:upload-or-archive-instantiation-update-protocol}, but it is
8638: not the current package branch.  If neither local bundle files nor a later external byte-equality coordinate are
8639: present, then the primary note retains only the public-source citation to the
8640: pinned newmath repository and the architectural claim in the short text; the
8641: supplemental support claims depending on bundle-local files are not accepted by
8642: this package.
8643: \end{proposition}
8644: 
8645: 
8646: \medskip
8647: \begin{proof}
8648: The bundle-dependent support claims are exactly the claims whose certificate
8649: surface names files under \path{review_bundle/}.  Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}
8650: lists the schema, current pass-record ledger, submission-interface map,
8651: primary-claim inventory, verifier script, and verifier log as bundle files.
8652: Proposition~\ref{prop:reviewable-source-snapshot-interface} uses the bundled
8653: source snapshot, and Proposition~\ref{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage}
8654: uses the bundled case snapshots.  Therefore, when \path{review_bundle/} is
8655: actually included as exact supplemental material with matching final digests,
8656: each such certificate surface lies in the submitted artifact boundary and is
8657: locally checkable in the sense of the finite schema verifier.  Until the external
8658: upload record specified in
8659: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record} exists, these
8660: bundle-backed statements are conditional support claims rather than independent
8661: public-access claims.
8662: 
8663: If the bundle is absent, those bundle-local paths are no longer in the submitted
8664: artifact boundary.  The definition of local checkability requires every named
8665: path, manifest row, or log to lie in the submitted artifact, the supplemental
8666: review bundle, or an explicit path-verified historical boundary.  No DOI or
8667: institutional archive identifier is claimed in this package, so an omitted bundle
8668: has no replacement surface unless an archival equivalent satisfying
8669: Audit record~\ref{audit:review-bundle-archive-equivalence} is provided.  If neither
8670: surface is present, the affected claims fail the evidence-surface check of
8671: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}.  What remains is only
8672: the public repository citation at the pinned commit and the short note's
8673: architectural thesis, not the bundle-backed support certification.
8674: \end{proof}
8675: 
8676: \begin{proposition}[Case-snapshot non-rerun boundary]
8677: \label{audit:case-snapshot-non-rerun-boundary}
8678: Under the hypotheses of
8679: Propositions~\ref{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses} and
8680: \ref{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage}, the four case-study rows cannot
8681: be read as fresh dynamic-behavior claims.  Any stronger reading asserting a fresh
8682: publication-daemon execution, full-source rebuild, theorem-content rerun,
8683: Rule110 dynamic rerun, or GroundCompiler semantic rerun requires command, source
8684: state, environment, exit-code, and log-path fields in addition to the bundled
8685: snapshots.  Without those fields the stronger reading is rejected by the finite
8686: record verifier at its evidence-strength boundary.
8687: \end{proposition}
8688: 
8689: 
8690: \medskip
8691: \begin{proof}
8692: The propositions identify the complete evidence surface of the case rows:
8693: thirteen local-path strings, thirteen reviewable bundle snapshots, and manifest
8694: boundaries recording historical or path-verified evidence.  None of those case
8695: rows contains the five command-run fields required by
8696: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.  A fresh daemon,
8697: full-source, theorem-content, Rule110, or GroundCompiler rerun is therefore a
8698: strictly stronger evidence-level assertion than the accepted row supplies.  By
8699: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} and
8700: the schema-relative finite-record exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, the same certificate cannot accept
8701: that stronger assertion: its boundary field $B$ records no command-run assertion,
8702: and the missing command tuple is a finite bad subrecord.  Thus the snapshots are
8703: rigidly limited to path-verified reviewability unless a separate rerun log is
8704: added.
8705: \end{proof}
8706: 
8707: \begin{theorem}[Finite-row closure of case evidence]
8708: \label{thm:finite-row-case-evidence-closure}
8709: Let $C$ be the finite set of four case-study rows in
8710: Table~\ref{tab:case-witnesses}.  Under
8711: Propositions~\ref{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses} and
8712: \ref{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage}, the publication-safety interface
8713: accepts the case-study contribution exactly as the image of $C$ under the
8714: skeleton map of Theorem~\ref{thm:case-witness-skeleton-rigidity}.  The image has
8715: four possible values only: promotion, route/submission blocking, theorem-content
8716: non-growth, and artifact-limitation disclosure.  For each row, the accepted
8717: claim is finite-record evidence for the corresponding software-workflow lesson;
8718: it is not evidence that the historical daemon, overlap checker, fake-extension
8719: pipeline, Rule110 artifact system, or GroundCompiler evaluator has been rerun or
8720: semantically validated.  A proposed upgrade of any row to such a rerun or
8721: semantic-validation claim is accepted if and only if it adds the positive
8722: extension coordinates required by
8723: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, including command, source
8724: state, environment, exit code, and log path whenever fresh execution is asserted.
8725: \end{theorem}
8726: 
8727: \begin{proof}
8728: Proposition~\ref{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses} gives, for every row in $C$,
8729: the gate name, observed issue, safe lesson, and cited path surface.  Proposition~\ref{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage}
8730: places those cited path surfaces inside the reviewable snapshot boundary and
8731: records that the boundary is historical or path-verified.  Applying
8732: Theorem~\ref{thm:case-witness-skeleton-rigidity} therefore maps each row to one
8733: of the four skeleton values listed in the statement, and no fifth skeleton is
8734: available because the table contains only those four gate/lesson forms.
8735: 
8736: The finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface},
8737: then accepts only the certificate tuple carried by the row: its claim kind, path
8738: surface, assigned pass rows, and boundary field.  By
8739: Proposition~\ref{audit:case-snapshot-non-rerun-boundary}, that boundary field has
8740: no command-run tuple for a fresh daemon execution, full-source rebuild,
8741: theorem-content rerun, Rule110 rerun, or GroundCompiler semantic rerun.  Hence the
8742: accepted image of $C$ is exactly finite-row evidence for the four safe workflow
8743: lessons and not an empirical rerun or full artifact-validation claim.
8744: 
8745: Conversely, suppose a stronger reading of a case row is accepted.  It changes at
8746: least one finite coordinate: the claim-kind coordinate for a semantic or artifact
8747: validation assertion, the evidence-surface coordinate for a new run or semantic
8748: property, the assigned-gate coordinate for the enlarged gate set, or the boundary
8749: coordinate for command-run strength.  The finite extension-budget normal form,
8750: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, says that exactly these
8751: positive coordinates must be added, and clause A6 of
8752: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} supplies the command,
8753: source-state, environment, exit-code, and log-path requirement for fresh
8754: execution.  If any required coordinate is absent, the schema exactness theorem,
8755: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, returns a finite bad subrecord
8756: instead of accepting the upgrade.  This proves both finite-row closure and the
8757: upgrade criterion.
8758: \end{proof}
8759: 
8760: \begin{corollary}[Current case-behaviour evidence criterion]
8761: \label{cor:current-case-behaviour-evidence-criterion}
8762: The four case-study rows support current-behaviour claims only for the finite
8763: record-verifier commands that are explicitly listed in
8764: Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory}.  They do not support current daemon,
8765: overlap-checker, fake-extension-pipeline, Rule110, GroundCompiler, or full
8766: artifact-suite behaviour unless the row is enlarged by a fresh command tuple for
8767: that system.  For each such current-behaviour upgrade, the required tuple is
8768: nonempty and contains at least command, source state, environment, exit code, log
8769: path, and the assigned pass row binding the run to the submitted support surface.
8770: \end{corollary}
8771: 
8772: \begin{proof}
8773: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-row-case-evidence-closure} identifies the accepted case
8774: image as the finite skeleton of four historical lessons and proves the exact
8775: upgrade criterion for rerun or semantic-validation readings.  The only command
8776: rows available before the case rows are the finite verifier rows displayed in
8777: Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory}: certificate-record verification,
8778: source-interface comparison, primary-claim inventory verification,
8779: theorem-environment extraction, digest generation, and dated venue/bibliography
8780: reachability.  None of these rows names a daemon execution, overlap-checker run,
8781: fake-extension-pipeline rerun, Rule110 rerun, GroundCompiler rerun, or full
8782: artifact-suite validation.
8783: 
8784: Therefore a proposed current-behaviour reading for any of those systems changes
8785: the command-boundary coordinate of the case row.  Clause A6 of
8786: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} requires the five
8787: command-run fields whenever such a command-result assertion is made, and
8788: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} requires the corresponding
8789: positive extension coordinate and pass row.  If that tuple is absent,
8790: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} returns it as the
8791: finite bad subrecord.  If it is present and assigned, the upgraded current-run
8792: claim is accepted exactly at the evidence level of that fresh row.
8793: \end{proof}
