8910: \begin{theorem}[Post-edit rerun metadata gate]
8911: \label{thm:post-edit-rerun-metadata-gate}
8912: Let $I$ be the pair consisting of \path{theorem_inventory.json} and
8913: \path{theorem_inventory.md}, let $L$ be the tuple of retained extractor and
8914: finite-record verifier logs named in Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory},
8915: and let $D$ be \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.  After any edit to
8916: \path{main.tex}, $I$, or a retained support record, a claim that the package has
8917: a post-inventory fixed point for the new bytes is accepted only if the submitted
8918: surface also contains, for each newly claimed rerun, its command, source commit
8919: or source-state description, environment, exit code, and log path, and if $D$ is
8920: regenerated after those logs exist.  If those metadata rows are absent, the same
8921: source text has only the conditional reading of
8922: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}: the fixed point is the
8923: finite coordinate that would be obtained by the ordered rerun, not a current-byte
8924: execution certificate for the edited package.
8925: \end{theorem}
8926: 
8927: \begin{proof}
8928: The operational evidence interface,
8929: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}, makes command-run status
8930: a finite coordinate: the command, source state, environment, exit code, and log
8931: path are part of the accepted record.  The digest-closure refresh criterion,
8932: Theorem~\ref{thm:digest-closure-refresh-criterion}, makes the byte digest another
8933: finite coordinate: after an inventory or support-record byte changes, the old
8934: digest row names the old byte string and cannot identify the edited state.
8935: 
8936: The tuple $(I,L,D)$ is therefore ordered data, not a self-updating invariant.  If
8937: the edited package includes fresh rows for the extractor, the retained
8938: finite-record verifiers, any retained dated venue check, and the final digest
8939: generator, then Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point} applies to
8940: those rows and supplies the fixed point.  If one of the metadata fields or the
8941: post-log digest regeneration is missing, two packages can agree on the manuscript
8942: prose while differing on the command actually run, the source state used, the
8943: environment, the exit status, the log bytes, or the final digest bytes.  By
8944: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, the stronger
8945: current-byte rerun claim is then not determined by the submitted surface; the
8946: missing command or digest field is the finite bad subrecord.  What remains is
8947: only the conditional finite-record statement, namely that such a fixed point is
8948: available once the ordered rerun metadata and final digest coordinate are
8949: supplied.
8950: \end{proof}
8951: 
8952: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record support-state reconciliation invariant]
8953: \label{thm:final-local-support-state-reconciliation}
8954: For the source package after the present manuscript edits, the finite support
8955: state used by the publication-safety theorem chain is the following reconciled
8956: local state and no stronger one.  First, the files
8957: \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md} are accepted as a
8958: post-inventory fixed point only under the ordered metadata condition of
8959: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-edit-rerun-metadata-gate}; absent such post-edit rows, the
8960: fixed-point statements are conditional rerun criteria rather than current-byte
8961: execution certificates.  Second, each command-run source state is the state
8962: recorded in the corresponding log row of Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory};
8963: historical rows are not claims that every later editor checkout has the same Git
8964: \path{HEAD} or digest bytes.  Third, because the source package records neither
8965: an upload receipt nor an archive-equivalent locator, bundle-backed support
8966: remains conditional on the external instantiation record of
8967: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}.
8968: 
8969: Moreover the same finite state is maximal simultaneously for the four boundary
8970: interfaces on which the presentation route depends:
8971: \begin{enumerate}
8972: \item the imported BEDC/newmath discovery-gate source interface is exactly the
8973: bounded path, commit, line-range, label, and summary interface of
8974: Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} and
8975: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality}; a full source-verification
8976: reading requires fresh Lean or BEDC build records, axiom-purity records, and
8977: source-marker or labelled-statement existence logs;
8978: \item the publication-safety and current-package claims are exactly the
8979: finite-record certificates of Theorems~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}
8980: and~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} under the explicitly supplied
8981: record-gate soundness interface, not implementation soundness of the gates; the
8982: current-package certificate is schema-relative and row-frozen to the declared
8983: verifier schema and the six current command rows, so semantic truth of package claims,
8984: post-edit freshness, and external venue status require the semantic
8985: non-escalation, command-boundary, and post-edit rerun coordinates named below;
8986: \item the four case-study rows are exactly the path-complete historical snapshot
8987: lessons classified by Theorem~\ref{thm:case-witness-skeleton-rigidity} and closed
8988: by Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-row-case-evidence-closure}; current operational
8989: reproducibility of any daemon, overlap checker, Rule110 system, GroundCompiler
8990: evaluator, or artifact suite requires the corresponding rerun log; and
8991: \item the theorem-obligation inventory is exactly the syntactic labelled
8992: environment coordinate on \path{main.tex} proved by
8993: Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary} and
8994: Theorem~\ref{thm:extractor-domain-exactness-non-gluing}; non-main \TeX{} files,
8995: snapshot excerpts, and auxiliary tables are supporting or background material
8996: unless separately brought into the extractor domain, and the coordinate is not a
8997: proof-correctness, semantic-theorem-completeness, Lean/BEDC, daemon, artifact, or
8998: venue certificate.
8999: \end{enumerate}
9000: Any stronger reading of one of these four interfaces is accepted by the
9001: publication-safety theorem chain if and only if the submitted support state adds
9002: the positive finite coordinates named in the relevant item and the assigned pass
9003: records required by Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}; if a
9004: required coordinate is absent, the attempted upgrade has an inclusion-minimal
9005: finite bad subrecord in the sense of
9006: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.
9007: \end{theorem}
9008: 
9009: \begin{proof}
9010: The first assertion is exactly the fixed-point criterion specialized in
9011: Proposition~\ref{prop:post-inventory-fixed-point-support-certificate} and
9012: realized by Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-finite-record-realization}: the two
9013: inventory paths and the verifier-log paths are finite byte coordinates of the
9014: selected exact-inclusion support surface.  If any of those rows is stale,
9015: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} rejects the stronger
9016: exact-inclusion claim and leaves only the local index reading.
9017: 
9018: For the second assertion, Proposition~\ref{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory}
9019: classifies the command-boundary table.  A row with command, source state,
9020: environment, exit code, and log path is accepted only at the evidence level
9021: stated in that row.  Hence a historical source-state field certifies the logged
9022: finite command result, not an equality between the final editor workspace and the
9023: historical commit.  This is the reconciliation theorem
9024: Theorem~\ref{thm:workspace-final-package-reconciliation} applied to the refreshed
9025: log rows.
9026: 
9027: For the third assertion,
9028: Proposition~\ref{prop:submission-support-instantiation-field-normal-form} lists
9029: the upload receipt and archive-equivalent locator fields as absent in the current
9030: package.  Corollary~\ref{cor:current-no-receipt-support-surface-non-escalation}
9031: therefore forbids promoting a bundle-local path into an unconditional venue or
9032: public support coordinate.  Combining the three finite fields gives precisely
9033: the local reconciled support state stated above, and no additional source-state,
9034: upload, archive, Lean, daemon, or artifact-rerun coordinate is introduced.
9035: 
9036: It remains to prove simultaneous maximality for the four interfaces.  For the
9037: source interface, Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} fixes
9038: the manual record fields, Proposition~\ref{prop:source-interface-evidence-level-separation}
9039: separates those fields from stronger formal-source evidence levels, and
9040: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality} proves maximality for the
9041: submitted source surface.  A fresh Lean or BEDC rebuild, axiom-purity assertion,
9042: or source-marker existence assertion reads a command, formal-build, axiom-audit,
9043: or marker-coordinate not present in the bounded line-range interface.  Hence the
9044: finite-coordinate theorem,
9045: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, forbids deriving it
9046: from the submitted fields alone, while
9047: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} gives the positive
9048: direction after the missing records and pass rows are added.
9049: 
9050: For the safety interface, Definition~\ref{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface}
9051: and Proposition~\ref{prop:record-gate-soundness-external-interface-exactness}
9052: fix record-gate soundness as an external finite input/output promise.  The finite
9053: publication theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, and the
9054: current package theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness},
9055: use that promise only to construct and identify finite publication certificates.
9056: Theorems~\ref{thm:external-interface-projection-no-free-upgrade} and
9057: \ref{thm:current-package-extension-coordinate-rigidity} classify an
9058: implementation-soundness reading as a proper extension requiring gate-source,
9059: command, environment, exit-code, and log coordinates.  Thus the stated finite
9060: safety chain is not demoted: it remains the maximal accepted theorem chain at the
9061: record level, and the stronger software-verification theorem is accepted exactly
9062: when its additional executable gate records are supplied.
9063: 
9064: For the case rows, Proposition~\ref{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses} provides
9065: the finite path-complete row surface, Theorem~\ref{thm:case-witness-skeleton-rigidity}
9066: classifies each row into one of the four skeleton lessons, and
9067: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-row-case-evidence-closure} proves closure under the
9068: finite-record interface.  A current operational reproduction claim changes the
9069: command-boundary coordinate: it asks for a fresh daemon, overlap-checker,
9070: Rule110, GroundCompiler, or artifact-suite run rather than for the historical
9071: snapshot lesson.  Proposition~\ref{audit:case-snapshot-non-rerun-boundary} shows
9072: that the required command tuple is absent, so the obstruction skeleton returns
9073: that missing tuple as a finite bad subrecord unless the rerun log and assigned
9074: pass rows are added.
9075: 
9076: For the theorem-inventory coordinate,
9077: Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary} proves the
9078: deterministic labelled-environment extraction predicate, and
9079: Theorem~\ref{thm:extractor-domain-exactness-non-gluing} proves that the domain is
9080: exactly the five displayed theorem-like environments in \path{main.tex}.  The
9081: manual boundary proposition,
9082: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary}, separately identifies
9083: non-main \TeX{} material as manual supporting or background material.  Therefore
9084: semantic theorem completeness, proof correctness, non-main-file coverage, Lean or
9085: BEDC rebuilding, daemon execution, artifact validation, and venue facts all read
9086: coordinates outside the extractor predicate.  The same finite-coordinate and
9087: extension-budget theorems then give non-escalation when those coordinates are
9088: absent and acceptance when they are explicitly added with assigned pass rows.
9089: 
9090: In all four cases the stronger reading preorder of
9091: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder} is strict exactly when the reading
9092: adds one of the named coordinates.  If the coordinate is absent,
9093: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} supplies an
9094: inclusion-minimal finite bad subrecord; if it is present with the required pass
9095: rows, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} supplies the upgraded
9096: finite certificate.  This proves the advertised simultaneous maximality and the
9097: ``if and only if'' upgrade criterion.
9098: \end{proof}
9099: 
9100: \begin{theorem}[Supplement-level presentation-route five-interface sharpness]